The Gherkin representation is more aligned with the acceptance criteria and user interactions, which is beneficial for behavioral testing. On the other hand, xUnit Test Cases are more appropriate for the actual code implementation and integration testing, which is crucial for the functional aspects of the system. Both representations are suitable, but xUnit Test Cases might offer a more direct way to validate the code against the requirements. The choice between them can also depend on the stage of development; Gherkin is often used earlier for clarity and communication, while xUnit Test Cases are used for implementation and regression testing. Given the need for both high-level specifications and low-level code validation, using both could be ideal, but if choosing one, xUnit Test Cases are more universally applicable for testing the code itself.
